; 09-use-an-existential-witness.kurt
;
; Use an existential witness
;
; `pick` opens a block around a fresh witness obtained from an existential fact. The conclusion
; leaving the block must not mention the witness.

load logic
bool P, B, C
arity P 1
const P, B, C

use forall $x (P($x) implies B)
use B implies C
use exists $x P($x)

show C
proof
    pick a with P(a)
        P(a) implies B
        B
    C
qed

; Inside the block, `a` is one particular but unknown witness and `P(a)` is available. Closing the
; block preserves `C` because `C` does not mention `a`.
;
; From an existential with a condition, `exists $x > 0 P($x)`, the fact about the witness is the
; condition and the body: `pick c with c > 0 and P(c)`, or just `pick c > 0 and P(c)` (the
; witness is the new name of the formula). `pick` needs the existential as a fact: to show
; something for an arbitrary `x` with a property instead, use `let x with ...` (lesson 08).
